course: dictionary
lesson: 12173
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Conception is a good example. «Her conception of the story changed when she read the last chapter.» Here conception means the idea she had in her mind, not a baby.
explain: Conception can also mean the start of a plan or an idea. «The class's conception of the science fair began with one question about bees.» Ask yourself: is this sentence about an idea, or about the very start of something?
explain: A misconception is a wrong idea. «Tom had a misconception that bats are blind, but they can see quite well.» The prefix mis- means wrong, so misconception means a wrongly understood idea. You can spot the same pattern in misunderstanding and mispronounce.
explain: In science lessons, conception has another meaning: it is the moment a new life begins to grow inside a mother's body. You will learn more about this as you grow older, and your teacher or family can answer any questions you have.
fact: Conception means the start of something, such as an idea, a plan, or a new life.
fact: «Conception» is a noun, and its plural is «conceptions».
fact: «Misconception» means a wrong idea about something.
fact: «A clear conception of» something means you understand it well.
fact: In science, conception means the moment a new life begins to grow in a mother's body.
exercise: «The inventors had a clear conception of how the robot would work.» What does conception mean here? | A type of robot toy | A plan that was thrown away | A baby being born | *An idea or understanding of how something works
exercise: Sara thought the moon was made of cheese. That is a misconception. What does misconception mean? | A correct idea that everyone agrees with | *A wrong idea | A kind of cheese | A very small moon
exercise: «From its conception, the school garden project was about helping bees.» What does «from its conception» mean? | From the weather report | From the end, when it was finished | From the day the bees left | *From the very start of the project
test: «The club's conception was a single idea shared at lunch.» Which word or phrase means the same as conception here? | *The start | A picture book | A lunch box | The ending
test: Ella says, «I had a misconception that sharks sleep in the sea.» What does she mean? | Sharks are very tired | She has never seen a shark | She knows exactly how sharks sleep | *She thought something was true when it was not
exam: «From conception to birth, every stage of growing up is amazing.» What does conception mean in this sentence? | A wrong idea | The end of a plan | *The start of a new life growing in a mother's body | A kind of party
